WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
SpaceX completed three Falcon launches, four first-stage landings, and a dragon docking to the station in under 13 hours. This is fascinating Frontiers, episode 211. Reusable rockets, private launch companies, and advances in manufacturing are lowering barriers to orbit, and expanding opportunities for space exploration. For decades, getting into space was an expensive and tightly controlled enterprise, largely limited to powerful nations and large defense organizations.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:36.680
that is changing rapidly. Reusable rockets, private launch companies, smaller launch vehicles,

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:42.100
and advances in manufacturing are bringing down the cost of reaching orbit and opening space

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:48.560
to a wider range of countries, companies, universities, and researchers. The theme for World Space Week

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:54.300
is selected each year by the World Space Week Association, in collaboration with the United Nations

00:00:54.300 --> 00:01:00.180
office for outer space affairs. The annual observance marks two milestones in the history of space

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:07.800
exploration. The launch of Sputnik I on October 4, 1957, and the coming into force of the outer

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:14.620
space treaty on October 10, 1967. Several technological shifts are driving this change,

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:21.220
from reusable launch systems and heavy lift rockets to dedicated small satellite launchers and advances

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:27.060
in propulsion and manufacturing. Reusability as the norm has been pioneered through commercial

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:33.060
vertical take-off and landing systems that transform rockets from single-use machines into reusable

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:39.160
launch vehicles, now moving to international collaboration. The European Space Agency will

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:46.400
participate in the 77th International Astronautical Congress, which opens on Monday 5th October and runs

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:53.060
until Friday 9th October in Antalya, Turkey. More than 6,000 participants are expected at the

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:58.040
Nest Congress and Exhibition Center. Under the theme, the world needs more space.

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:06.000
ESA will be present throughout the event in Hall 1, Booth 1B02, with an exhibition showcasing

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:12.440
the agency's latest mission results, achievements, and future ambitions. Our presence at IAC aims

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:17.560
to reinforce the ESA's role as a global space leader and trusted international partner,

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:21.800
says Anne Sophie Bridel, head of ESA Communication Department.

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:27.520
Bilateral events, technical sessions and strategic initiatives taking place throughout the

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:33.560
week will reinforce ESA's commitment to international cooperation, advancing sustainable and

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:38.940
responsible exploration, and supporting ambitious missions from Earth to the Moon and beyond.

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:45.240
The exhibition, titled European Space Agency, elevating the future of Europe, highlights

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:51.200
the five goals of ESA's strategy, 2014, shifting to sky-watching opportunities.

