00:08:19.660 --> 00:08:25.440
Imagine a single piece of debris the size of a paint chip traveling at 17,000 miles per hour,

00:08:25.940 --> 00:08:28.500
striking a satellite the way a bullet strikes glass.

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:32.360
Low Earth orbit now holds millions of such fragments.

00:08:32.360 --> 00:08:36.920
each one a potential trigger for further collisions that multiply the hazard.

00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:48.160
A space-based laser station could fire short pulses at these objects, nudging them into slightly lower orbits where atmospheric drag finishes the job in weeks rather than centuries.

00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:56.980
One proposed system would need only a few hundred watts of power per pulse, yet could handle dozens of targets per pass over a ground station.

00:08:56.980 --> 00:08:59.540
